+# Agent Note: Electron IPC desktop client
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-13-electron-ipc-desktop-client.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The shipped GUI composition was a browser deployment: a Node HTTP server served the React shell and client bundles, `/api` carried unary RPC, and two WebSockets carried Host events. A desktop client could have embedded that URL, but doing so would retain a listening network service, browser reachability policy, and server lifecycle even though the Renderer and Host live on the same machine. Reimplementing the GUI for Electron would instead split the Client plugin graph and make desktop behavior drift from Web.
+
+The desktop shell also needed native directory selection and Session save behavior without giving the Renderer Node.js access, filesystem paths, or an unrestricted generic IPC surface. Native dependencies had to run under Electron's ABI without rewriting the source checkout's dependency installation.
+
+## Decision
+
+Electron's main process boots the ordinary profile launcher with a shipped `desktop` profile. That profile composes `dsh-base`, `dsh-web-app`, then `dsh-electron-app`; the final layer retains the Web Client roster and Host APIs, disables every HTTP/Web runtime row and client HMR, selects Connection's `electron` transport, and replaces the browse directory flow with the native Host and Client halves. The Host and Renderer remain one product composition even though their physical carrier changes.
+
+The main process owns the Host and exposes a fixed preload API. `boot()` returns `ClientModuleRegistry.graph()` before the shell starts. Unary and generic RPC serialize Fetch-shaped requests through IPC to Connection's shared Host handler. The `events.mux` and `events.host` sources are pumped into two generation-owned IPC streams with explicit open, frame, end, error, cancellation, and disposal states. The browser-safe `ElectronApiClient` implements the same `AbstractApiClient` boundary as Web, so the existing Connection controller and every business client plugin remain unchanged.
+
+The privileged standard scheme `dsh://app/` serves the built shell, extensionless SPA fallback routes, and only client bundles resolved by the live Host registry. It replaces `file://`: the Renderer gets a stable secure origin, Fetch-compatible resource loading, a Content Security Policy, and path containment. The Web boot kernel reads its graph from `window.__DSH_BOOT__` in a browser or `window.dshDesktop.boot()` in Electron.
+
+The preload is sandbox-compatible CommonJS and exposes only the typed bridge through `contextBridge`. Main-process handlers verify the exact BrowserWindow, main frame, and `dsh://app/` origin, then validate bounded request ids, methods, paths, headers, and bodies. The window has context isolation, no Node integration or WebView, denied permissions and child windows, and navigation restricted to the application origin.
+
+The BrowserWindow is frameless but retains the Windows thick frame for native edge resizing, shadow, and animations. The Web shell owns a compact title bar around both the boot page and settled UI, projects the same session-aware document title, loads the repository Logo from the built shell, and marks its non-control area as an Electron drag region. Operating-system dragging, snapping, double-click maximize/restore, and the title-bar system menu therefore remain native. A fixed `windowControls` bridge subset performs minimize, maximize/restore, and close operations and publishes maximize and fullscreen state; these channels use the same trusted-frame check as every other desktop IPC entry point.
+
+Native directory selection remains a Host capability. Session export is the desktop-specific publication action on the bridge: the main process opens a native save dialog, streams the existing Host ZIP response into a random sibling file, fsyncs it, and atomically renames it. Closing through either native window chrome semantics or the custom control removes IPC entry points, aborts and joins requests, streams, and exports, then disposes the Cordis tree before destroying the window.
+
+The runtime builder creates an injected production pnpm deployment below `.artifacts`, installs Electron only in that copy, rejects links escaping the runtime, and proves native compatibility by loading `koffi` and spawning a real `node-pty` child under the deployed Electron executable. It does not mutate workspace `node_modules` or claim an unobserved native rebuild.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Embed the existing Web URL and keep the HTTP/WebSocket server.** Rejected because the desktop application would still expose and govern a network surface, duplicate server shutdown concerns, and depend on a free port despite having a process-local Host.
+
+**Enable Node integration in the Renderer and run Host modules there.** Rejected because UI code and dynamically loaded Client plugins would inherit filesystem and process authority, collapse the Host/Client package boundary, and make a Renderer compromise equivalent to unrestricted Host code execution.
+
+**Create an Electron-only UI or fork each Client plugin.** Rejected because transport does not change business projections or interactions. A second UI graph would double lifecycle, localization, and behavioral maintenance for no domain benefit.
+
+**Load the shell with `file://`.** Rejected because opaque/local-file origin behavior is a poor security and routing boundary, SPA and plugin asset resolution become special cases, and the intended application origin cannot be validated consistently at IPC entry points.
+
+**Expose a generic IPC invoke or raw Host object.** Rejected because it would make privilege review open-ended. The fixed bridge mirrors the existing Connection contract and adds only Boot Graph discovery, enumerated native-window operations, and native Session publication.
+
+**Rebuild native modules in the source workspace.** Rejected because Electron ABI artifacts would contaminate ordinary Node development. An automatic fallback was also rejected once pnpm's injected nested layout showed that a rebuild tool could report success after finding no module; an executed native probe is the packaging authority.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Desktop and Web share the same React shell, Client plugin graph, Host API schemas, durable Session behavior, and profile override model. Electron starts no listening socket, requires no port selection, and can provide native chooser, save, and window-chrome flows without exposing Node.js to the Renderer. The desktop title bar is shell-owned presentation rather than a business plugin, so it also remains available while plugin boot is loading or reporting failure.
+
+The main process is now both platform shell and Host failure domain. IPC validation, cancellation, and disposal are security and correctness boundaries that must evolve with the shared Connection protocol. Client HMR is intentionally absent, so desktop development consumes rebuilt artifacts. Distribution still needs platform packaging, signing, and update policy, and a deployed Electron version must have compatible `node-pty` and `koffi` binaries before the runtime can be published.
