+# Agent Note: In-app Workspace file workbench
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-14-workspace-file-workbench.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Tool results can identify code, Markdown, and other files produced inside a Workspace, but handing every path to the operating system interrupts review and requires a separately installed editor. The desktop client needs to inspect and revise these artifacts without giving its Electron Renderer direct filesystem access. Saving inside the client must also preserve an external editor's concurrent changes, and a path displayed by a Tool must not become authority to read outside the registered Workspace.
+
+## Decision
+
+Selecting a regular produced file associated with the current Session's registered Workspace opens the existing details column in workbench mode. Directories, the Workspace root, and paths with no matching Workspace retain the existing `host.openPath` behavior. `DetailsHost` routes the shared details seat between Tool material and `FileWorkbench`. The layout keeps compact Tool details on their existing 300–520px range with a 640px conversation floor, while workbench mode opens at 720px, retains a 480px minimum without an artificial maximum, and may consume all frame width to the right of the sidebar while the conversation concedes to zero. On narrow frames, the workbench takes the complete application frame.
+
+`WorkspaceFilesRuntime` owns open documents, active selection, drafts, view modes, version tokens, and asynchronous operation state in the React-free object layer. Closing the column, switching Sessions, or selecting Tool details deactivates presentation without deleting documents or drafts. `FileWorkbench` keeps every open document mounted as a CodeMirror tab while the workbench is visible, so tabs retain independent editing history. Markdown starts in preview mode and offers source, preview, and split modes through the existing Markdown renderer; source mode selects CodeMirror language extensions by common suffix, including C and C++ source and header files. Closing or reloading a dirty document requires explicit confirmation, and opening the same Workspace path selects its existing tab.
+
+`ProjectExplorer` adds a toggleable project tree rooted at the active document's registered Workspace. It loads one directory at a time through the Workspace runtime, sorts directories before files, and filters only entries that have already been loaded; it never recursively scans the project. Clicking a file reuses the ordinary open-document path and existing tab. Refresh clears tree-local expansion and cached listings without touching open documents or drafts.
+
+The Host exposes bounded `workspace.listDirectory`, `workspace.readFile`, and `workspace.writeFile` RPC methods instead of a general Renderer filesystem bridge. All three resolve the registered Workspace root and requested path through the active `ctx.fs` provider and require provider-canonical containment. Directory listing omits special entries and symbolic-link or junction escapes. File operations reject traversal, symbolic-link and junction escapes, directories, binary content, unreadable files, and UTF-8 content larger than 2 MiB. The Web carrier treats all three operations as loopback-only; Electron reaches the same typed methods through its fixed Connection bridge.
+
+A successful read returns complete text, byte size, canonical display path, and an opaque filesystem version. Save carries the last successful read or write version and calls the filesystem provider with `replaceIfVersion`; a stale version returns `workspace-file-conflict` and keeps the local draft unchanged. Successful writes atomically replace the file and advance the version. The workbench never silently chooses between a local draft and externally changed content; reload is the explicit discard path.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Continue opening every artifact in an external application.** Rejected because it cannot provide an integrated review, multi-file editing, or Markdown preview workflow and makes the desktop client dependent on separately configured applications for ordinary generated output.
+
+**Expose Node.js or unrestricted filesystem IPC to the Electron Renderer.** Rejected because dynamically loaded Client plugins would inherit Host filesystem authority. A fixed Workspace RPC pair keeps path resolution, policy, size limits, and writes in the Host process.
+
+**Save with last-writer-wins replacement.** Rejected because a client draft based on an older read could silently erase changes made by a terminal, another editor, or another client. Version-guarded replacement turns that race into a visible conflict while preserving both contents.
+
+**Keep one editor's state inside the React component tree.** Rejected because details-column collapse, Session navigation, and Tool-detail routing would discard drafts or require presentation state to become business data. The object layer owns documents, while retained CodeMirror instances own editor-specific history.
+
+## Verification
+
+Host tests exercise lazy directory listings, successful versioned reads and writes, plus traversal, symbolic-link and junction escape, binary, oversized, non-regular, and stale-version refusals. Runtime tests pin directory-list forwarding, multi-tab drafts, reload, cancellation, version advancement, and conflict preservation. Component and browser tests exercise tree expansion, filtering, refresh, file selection, real CodeMirror transactions, C/C++ syntax highlighting, undo/redo, keyboard save, Markdown preview and split modes, external opening, unrestricted wide-frame resizing, responsive workbench layout, and saving a real Workspace Markdown artifact.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Generated code and Markdown can be reviewed and edited without leaving the shared Web or Electron client, while the operating system handoff remains available as an explicit secondary action. The same Host security and concurrency rules apply to both carriers, and no file content enters a durable Session event or a persisted UI store.
+
+The workbench intentionally edits only existing, bounded text files; it does not create, rename, delete, merge, or format files. Binary and larger artifacts still require an external application. Unsaved drafts survive in-app navigation but not a process restart, conflict resolution is reload-based rather than a merge UI, and CodeMirror language support increases the Client bundle and Electron runtime size.
