+# Agent Note: Workspace path references in conversation input
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-17-workspace-path-references.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Users can discuss a Workspace file or directory by typing its path, but manual paths are easy to mistype and the composer has no Workspace-aware discovery. Attaching file contents would solve a different problem: a path reference needs to identify an artifact without spending context on its contents or granting the Renderer broader filesystem access.
+
+## Decision
+
+The Workspace Client plugin registers two `@` input-trigger sources named `workspace-file` and `workspace-folder`. They appear as `file` and `folder` commands only when the addressed Session belongs to a registered Workspace. Selecting a command inserts the searchable `@file:` or `@folder:` prefix; selecting a result creates a `ReferenceInsert` whose source-scoped value is the raw Workspace-relative path. Clipboard projection and submit serialization both produce the same literal reference. Paths containing whitespace or quotes use a JSON-quoted path.
+
+The Client builds a path-only index by recursively calling the existing bounded `workspace.listDirectory` runtime method. It never calls `workspace.readFile`. A scan skips version-control metadata, prioritizes ordinary source directories over dependency and build trees, lists up to six directories concurrently, and stops after 20,000 indexed paths. Query changes share the in-flight per-Workspace scan, while reopening an empty prefix refreshes a settled index. Local fuzzy ranking orders exact basenames, basename prefixes, path prefixes, containment, and subsequence matches; each source returns at most 40 results.
+
+Both Web and Electron use the same Client plugin and Workspace runtime method. Web carries listings through its existing loopback transport and Electron through its existing fixed IPC bridge, so the feature adds no Host RPC or Renderer filesystem authority. Durable user bubbles recognize both serialized path forms as reference chips without changing their copy text.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Attach or inject the selected file contents.** Rejected because the feature identifies an artifact rather than requesting its contents. Automatic reads would increase prompt size, make directory semantics ambiguous, and couple a path mention to an implicit context policy.
+
+**Add a recursive Host search RPC.** Rejected because the existing directory-listing method already enforces Workspace containment and is available through both transports. Client-side indexing keeps fuzzy ranking and interaction caching in the owning UI plugin without widening Host authority.
+
+**Insert plain text without a reference occurrence.** Rejected because a `ReferenceInsert` preserves one identity for chip rendering, clipboard projection, undo, persistence, and asynchronous submit serialization. The model still receives plain path text; the occurrence exists only in composer state.
+
+**Ship a separate Workspace-reference package.** Rejected because the source depends on the Workspace list and directory runtime already owned by `ui-workspace`, has no independent service, and must follow the same plugin lifecycle. A separate package would add composition and dependency edges without creating an independently replaceable behavior.
+
+## Verification
+
+Workspace Client tests cover command discovery, Session-to-Workspace selection, recursive file and folder filtering, ranking, quoting, copy and model serialization, scan sharing, refresh, cancellation, disposal, inaccessible directories, VCS exclusion, deferred dependency traversal, result and index bounds, and the absence of file reads. Plugin assembly tests pin source registration and teardown. Conversation rendering tests pin durable chips and unchanged copy text.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Users can discover and mention Workspace artifacts from either Web or Electron while sending only stable relative paths. Large Workspaces pay one bounded recursive scan per opened prefix, and newly generated paths appear after reopening the prefix. The 20,000-path bound means dependency-heavy trees can omit later entries; content search, file attachment, and automatic reads remain separate features.
