+# `@deepseek-ai/dsh-electron`
+
+English | [中文](README.zh.md)
+
+The DeepSeek Harness desktop shell. The Electron main process boots the `desktop` profile and its Host in the same process, while the sandboxed renderer reuses the built React Web shell and supplies the frameless window's title bar. Requests, native window controls, and the two Host event streams cross a narrow preload IPC bridge; the desktop application opens no HTTP or WebSocket listener.
+
+## Run from a checkout
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```powershell
+pnpm run build:electron
+pnpm run start:electron
+```
+
+`build:electron` first builds the Host, Client, and Web artifacts, then creates an isolated production deployment at `.artifacts/electron-runtime`. The deploy does not run dependency lifecycle scripts or modify the source workspace's `node_modules`; it installs the deployed Electron binary, verifies that every link remains inside the runtime, and starts Electron in Node mode to load `koffi` and create a real `node-pty` process. An incompatible native prebuild fails with an actionable diagnostic rather than mutating the workspace or claiming that an empty rebuild succeeded.
+
+Use `--skip-build` to reuse current repository artifacts, or `--output=.artifacts/<name>` to select another runtime directory. Output is restricted to descendants of `.artifacts`.
+
+`start:electron` deliberately invokes the deployed Electron CLI without changing the caller's working directory. `process.cwd()` is the default workspace and the root used to discover the project `.env`, so launch the command from the project the desktop client should initially use. Harness state defaults to `~/.dsh` and honors `DSH_HOME`.
+
+## Process boundary
+
+| Layer | Ownership |
+|---|---|
+| Main process | Boots the `desktop` profile, owns the Host, native dialogs, Session ZIP publication, the `dsh` protocol, and application teardown. |
+| Preload | Exposes only `window.dshDesktop`: Boot Graph read, bounded unary requests, two cancellable event streams, Session export, and fixed native-window controls. |
+| Renderer | Runs the existing Web shell and client plugin graph with no Node.js integration, including the shell-owned custom title bar. |
+
+The renderer obtains the Host-composed Boot Graph through `dshDesktop.boot()`. The privileged `dsh://app/` protocol serves the built shell, SPA fallbacks, and exact client-plugin bundles. Connection selects its Electron carrier automatically when the preload bridge is present: unary and generic RPC use the Host's shared Fetch handler, while `events.mux` and `events.host` are pumped directly from `ApiProxy` to renderer IPC.
+
+The BrowserWindow removes native chrome but retains the Windows thick frame for edge resizing, shadow, and window animations. The Web shell renders the repository Logo, current session-aware title, and native-semantic minimize, maximize/restore, and close buttons above both the loading and settled UI. Its non-control area is an Electron drag region, so operating-system dragging, snapping, double-click maximize/restore, and the title-bar system menu remain native behaviors; maximize and fullscreen events update the renderer state.
+
+The desktop profile is `dsh-base` + `dsh-web-app` + [`dsh-electron-app`](../../packages/bundle/electron-app/README.md). Its final patch disables the Web server, Web startup/runtime glue, browser directory picker, and client HMR; selects `transport: electron`; and mounts the native OS directory picker.
+
+## Security and lifecycle
+
+The renderer uses `sandbox: true`, `contextIsolation: true`, `nodeIntegration: false`, and no WebView. The main process denies permission requests, new windows, WebView attachment, and navigation outside `dsh://app/`. The custom protocol sets a restrictive Content Security Policy and contains static-path resolution within the built frontend or exact registered plugin bundle.
+
+Every IPC handler verifies the exact BrowserWindow, main frame, and `dsh://app/` origin before dispatch, including window-control messages. Request ids, paths, methods, headers, and bodies are validated and bounded in the main process. Synthetic internal requests carry a loopback Host authority, so Host methods retain the same privileged-method fence without exposing a network endpoint.
+
+Closing the window, quitting the application, or losing the renderer removes IPC handlers first, aborts active requests, streams, and exports, awaits their settlement, disposes the Cordis tree, unregisters the protocol, and then exits. Session exports use a native save dialog and stream to a random sibling file before an atomic rename, so cancellation or failure never publishes a partial ZIP.
+
+## Known Limitations and Deferred Work
+
+- **No distributable installer yet** — code signing, platform packaging, installer generation, and automatic updates are outside this runtime build.
+- **One application window** — the IPC trust and lifecycle owner are intentionally bound to one BrowserWindow.
+- **Built client artifacts only** — desktop client HMR is disabled; rebuild the runtime after frontend or client-plugin changes.
+- **Native prebuilds must support the deployed Electron ABI** — the builder verifies `node-pty` and `koffi` but does not compile a fallback toolchain on the user's machine.
